{
 "fps": 30,
 "lead": 0.8,
 "tail": 2.0,
 "footer": "Sources: 2026 federal, Alberta, Ontario, Manitoba rates \u00b7 GST/HST/RST general rates \u00b7 Example calculation, general info, not advice",
 "vo": [
  {
   "id": "L1",
   "file": "vo/L1.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three people. Same job. Same salary: one hundred thousand dollars. One lives in Calgary, one in Toronto, one in Winnipeg."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2",
   "file": "vo/L2.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Every payday, the same race. Federal tax, provincial tax, C P P, E I."
  },
  {
   "id": "L3",
   "file": "vo/L3.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "By the end of the year, one of them is three thousand dollars ahead of last place. And Toronto finishes just two hundred and fifty-three dollars behind the leader."
  },
  {
   "id": "L4",
   "file": "vo/L4.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But the paycheque isn't the finish line. There's a second race, at the checkout, and a third one when the salary goes up. By the end, the gap looks very different."
  },
  {
   "id": "L5",
   "file": "vo/L5.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "All numbers: twenty twenty-six rates, a single employee, no other income. Our example, not a real person. General information, not advice."
  },
  {
   "id": "L6",
   "file": "vo/L6.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "First, what's the same everywhere."
  },
  {
   "id": "L7",
   "file": "vo/L7.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Federal tax on one hundred thousand dollars is identical in all three provinces: thirteen thousand, three hundred and two dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L8",
   "file": "vo/L8.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "C P P: four thousand, six hundred and forty-six. E I: one thousand, one hundred and twenty-three. Same in Calgary, Toronto and Winnipeg."
  },
  {
   "id": "L9",
   "file": "vo/L9.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "That's nineteen thousand dollars that doesn't care where you live. The entire race is decided by one line: provincial tax."
  },
  {
   "id": "L10",
   "file": "vo/L10.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So, round one. Provincial income tax on the same hundred thousand dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L11",
   "file": "vo/L11.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Alberta: six thousand, four hundred and seventy dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L12",
   "file": "vo/L12.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Ontario: six thousand, seven hundred and twenty-three. That already includes the Ontario Health Premium, seven hundred and fifty dollars, and a surtax of about twenty-six."
  },
  {
   "id": "L13",
   "file": "vo/L13.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Manitoba: nine thousand, four hundred and eighty-four."
  },
  {
   "id": "L14",
   "file": "vo/L14.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Ontario is only two hundred and fifty-three dollars behind Alberta. Manitoba is more than three thousand behind."
  },
  {
   "id": "L15",
   "file": "vo/L15.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "How can Ontario be that close? Two different strategies."
  },
  {
   "id": "L16",
   "file": "vo/L16.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Alberta starts higher, at eight percent, but it has a big basic personal amount: twenty-two thousand, seven hundred and sixty-nine dollars of income that the credit covers."
  },
  {
   "id": "L17",
   "file": "vo/L17.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Ontario starts low: five point oh five percent on the first fifty-three thousand, eight hundred and ninety-one dollars. Then nine point one five percent."
  },
  {
   "id": "L18",
   "file": "vo/L18.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "At one hundred thousand dollars, those two designs land in almost the same place. Higher up, they don't. Remember that."
  },
  {
   "id": "L19",
   "file": "vo/L19.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And Manitoba? Ten point eight percent to forty-seven thousand dollars, then twelve point seven five. Higher rates, from the very first bracket."
  },
  {
   "id": "L20",
   "file": "vo/L20.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Look at the next steps up. Alberta stays at ten percent until one hundred and fifty-four thousand, two hundred and fifty-nine dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L21",
   "file": "vo/L21.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Ontario moves to eleven point one six percent above one hundred and seven thousand, seven hundred and eighty-five, and twelve point one six above one hundred and fifty thousand. Plus the surtax on top."
  },
  {
   "id": "L22",
   "file": "vo/L22.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Manitoba jumps to seventeen point four percent above one hundred thousand dollars. Our example earns exactly one hundred thousand, so Manitoba's top bracket hasn't even started yet."
  },
  {
   "id": "L23",
   "file": "vo/L23.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Photo finish. Take-home pay for the year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L24",
   "file": "vo/L24.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Calgary: seventy-four thousand, four hundred and fifty-nine dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L25",
   "file": "vo/L25.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Toronto: seventy-four thousand, two hundred and six."
  },
  {
   "id": "L26",
   "file": "vo/L26.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Winnipeg: seventy-one thousand, four hundred and forty-five."
  },
  {
   "id": "L27",
   "file": "vo/L27.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Per month: about six thousand, two hundred and five in Calgary, six thousand, one hundred and eighty-four in Toronto, and five thousand, nine hundred and fifty-four in Winnipeg."
  },
  {
   "id": "L28",
   "file": "vo/L28.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Calgary and Toronto: twenty-one dollars a month apart. Winnipeg: about two hundred and fifty a month behind."
  },
  {
   "id": "L29",
   "file": "vo/L29.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But the money doesn't stay in the bank account. It gets spent. And that's race number two."
  },
  {
   "id": "L30",
   "file": "vo/L30.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Sales tax at the checkout. In Alberta, only the federal G S T: five percent."
  },
  {
   "id": "L31",
   "file": "vo/L31.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In Manitoba, five percent G S T plus seven percent retail sales tax. Twelve percent."
  },
  {
   "id": "L32",
   "file": "vo/L32.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In Ontario, the harmonized sales tax: thirteen percent."
  },
  {
   "id": "L33",
   "file": "vo/L33.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "On every thousand dollars of taxable purchases: fifty dollars in Calgary, a hundred and twenty in Winnipeg, a hundred and thirty in Toronto."
  },
  {
   "id": "L34",
   "file": "vo/L34.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Now, not everything is taxed. Basic groceries and many other items aren't taxed, and what's taxable isn't always the same in each province. So let's use an illustration: twenty thousand dollars a year of purchases taxed at the general rate."
  },
  {
   "id": "L35",
   "file": "vo/L35.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Sales tax: one thousand dollars in Calgary. Two thousand, four hundred in Winnipeg. Two thousand, six hundred in Toronto."
  },
  {
   "id": "L36",
   "file": "vo/L36.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Subtract it, and the photo finish isn't close anymore. Calgary keeps seventy-three thousand, four hundred and fifty-nine. Toronto, seventy-one thousand, six hundred and six. Winnipeg, sixty-nine thousand and forty-five."
  },
  {
   "id": "L37",
   "file": "vo/L37.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The Calgary-Toronto gap goes from two hundred and fifty-three dollars to about eighteen hundred and fifty. Calgary-Winnipeg: about forty-four hundred."
  },
  {
   "id": "L38",
   "file": "vo/L38.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Your number depends on what you buy. The more you spend on taxable things, the more the checkout matters."
  },
  {
   "id": "L39",
   "file": "vo/L39.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Race number three. What if the salary isn't a hundred thousand?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L40",
   "file": "vo/L40.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "At sixty thousand dollars: Calgary keeps forty-seven thousand, six hundred and ninety-one. Toronto, forty-seven thousand, three hundred and forty. Winnipeg, forty-five thousand, seven hundred and seventy-two."
  },
  {
   "id": "L41",
   "file": "vo/L41.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Calgary and Toronto still about three hundred and fifty apart. Close."
  },
  {
   "id": "L42",
   "file": "vo/L42.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Now one hundred and fifty thousand. Calgary: one hundred and seven thousand, four hundred and fifty-eight. Toronto: one hundred and four thousand, three hundred and twenty. Winnipeg: one hundred thousand, seven hundred and ninety-seven."
  },
  {
   "id": "L43",
   "file": "vo/L43.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The Toronto gap just jumped to more than three thousand, one hundred dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L44",
   "file": "vo/L44.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's the two designs splitting apart. Ontario's higher brackets and its surtax start working above about a hundred thousand. Alberta stays at ten percent all the way to one hundred and fifty-four thousand."
  },
  {
   "id": "L45",
   "file": "vo/L45.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Winnipeg is behind at every salary: about nineteen hundred at sixty thousand, three thousand at one hundred, and about sixty-six hundred at one hundred and fifty."
  },
  {
   "id": "L46",
   "file": "vo/L46.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One more test. A raise. Give each of them one thousand dollars more, at one hundred thousand."
  },
  {
   "id": "L47",
   "file": "vo/L47.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Calgary keeps six hundred and ninety-five. Toronto, six hundred and eighty-five. Winnipeg, six hundred and sixty-eight."
  },
  {
   "id": "L48",
   "file": "vo/L48.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Small differences per raise. But they repeat on every dollar, every year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L49",
   "file": "vo/L49.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And the same logic flips for deductions. Say each of them puts ten thousand dollars into an R R S P, assuming they have the room."
  },
  {
   "id": "L50",
   "file": "vo/L50.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "At one hundred thousand dollars, the tax saved is about three thousand and fifty in Calgary, two thousand, nine hundred and ninety-one in Toronto, and three thousand, three hundred and twenty-five in Winnipeg."
  },
  {
   "id": "L51",
   "file": "vo/L51.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The highest-tax province gives the biggest reward for a deduction. Higher rates cut both ways."
  },
  {
   "id": "L52",
   "file": "vo/L52.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "At one hundred and fifty thousand, it shifts again: about three thousand, six hundred in Calgary, and about four thousand, three hundred and forty in both Toronto and Winnipeg."
  },
  {
   "id": "L53",
   "file": "vo/L53.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Now, the part a tax calculator can't tell you."
  },
  {
   "id": "L54",
   "file": "vo/L54.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Rent, home prices, car insurance, and salaries for the same job are all different in Calgary, Toronto and Winnipeg. Those can be bigger than every number in this video."
  },
  {
   "id": "L55",
   "file": "vo/L55.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So the tax race tells you one thing clearly: how much of the same salary the government takes. It doesn't tell you where life costs less."
  },
  {
   "id": "L56",
   "file": "vo/L56.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So, who wins?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L57",
   "file": "vo/L57.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "On the paycheque at one hundred thousand: Calgary, by a nose over Toronto. Winnipeg third."
  },
  {
   "id": "L58",
   "file": "vo/L58.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "At the checkout: Calgary pulls away, because there's no provincial sales tax."
  },
  {
   "id": "L59",
   "file": "vo/L59.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "At higher salaries: the Calgary-Toronto gap grows. At every salary, Winnipeg pays the most income tax of the three."
  },
  {
   "id": "L60",
   "file": "vo/L60.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And before anyone packs a moving truck: price the housing and the job in the new city first."
  },
  {
   "id": "L61",
   "file": "vo/L61.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Next on this channel: you start a side hustle. How much of every extra dollar do you actually keep? Subscribe, so you don't miss it."
  }
 ],
